<> Pablo de La Fuente Sancho, 2025
<> Monom 0.1


<> This is a single line comment
</
    This is a multiline comment.
    </
        It can be nested.
    />
/>

::: This is a single line doc comment.
:/
    This is a multiline doc comment.
    :/
        It can be nested.
    /:
/:
::: end_doc <> if we do not want to document, we can use this to ignore the docs above

::: Doc(file) This is a Monom file (.mnm) showing the syntax of the 0.1 version of the language.


::: Entry point for an executable
main() = (

    <><><> declarations (constant by default)

    d1 = 1
    d2: Int32 = 2
    def d3: Int64 <> definition
        </
            Takes the default value until assigned. For integers it is -1, for naturals 1, for floats -0.125,
            and for a new type, you can override __defaultValue__. The values are chosen so that they represent the type,
            so -1 for integers to differentiate them from naturals (1), aka unsigned ints in other languages, and
            -0.125 for floats to show the number has decimal digits and that it can be negative. The default value is not 0
            to be able to tell the difference. The maximum, minimum and these other things are atributes from the type, like
            Nat64.maxValue or Flot32.minValueOverZero for typical numeric types. You also have, for instance, in Float32,
            Float32.float32NaN, and the methods .is_NaN() and .is_not_NaN(), to manage NaN (not a number).
        />
    d3 = 3 <> then, we can assign the value for the defined identifier

    ::: Let's create an alias so that we don't need to write so much. This text is its documentation.
    use Int = Int64

    ::: another alias, to unlink the function from the namespace so that we write less
    use std.io.stdOut.print <> the last field is the introduced alias, equivalent to write use print = std.io.stdOut.print

    <> prefixes can be either before or in the type of an identifier (it is preferable in the type, when it is complex)
    <> const is not needed, since it is the default, but we will use it for the examples before introducing other prefixes
    <> local makes the identifier local bounded, therefore unreachable from an inner scope (private, the default, allows flowing down to inner scopes)
    <> local identifier from functions can not be used for closures of the scope, along other restrictions which might be helpful sometimes

    ::: the prefix before the data type
    d4: const Int = 4

    ::: no type is required to write the prefix
    d5: local = 5

    ::: you can write the prefix before the identifier
    local const d6 = 6

    ::: a mix of them in different positions is allowed too
    const d7: local = 7

    ::: the type, as expected, is written the same way, both with or without prefixes
    local d8: const Int = 8

    ::: and this is valid too
    local d9: Int = 9


    <><><> functions

    ::: 0 arguments
    f1() = print("this is f1\n")
    <> also valid as f1(none) = print("this is f1")

    ::: 1 argument
    f2(x) = x + 1

    <> f(x) + y is equivalent to (f(x)) + y

    f3(x) = x * 321

    nothing <> type Nothing (with value nothing), which can be ignored

    <> 3 this can not be ignored, it is not nothing, it is a number

    std.io.stdOut.println "Hello World!" <> returns nothing, so the result can be ignored

    <> to ignore something that is not nothing, i.e. something (simply called thing, from Thing), the do word is used
    f1() <> returns nothing
    do f2(6531112)
    do f3(5)

    ::: the body ends with ; (EOL), but if a declaration, a definition, use, import, export, a do statement... follows, the next expression is included
    f4(x) = <> since the equal expects a expression and it is not complete, EOL is skipped
        use std.io.stdOut .readln, .println <> a use expression to unlink readln and println from their namespace, shortening the path; EOL skipped

        ; <> equivalent to EOL, it is not skipped, just ; ; is ;, so it is considered the same as the last (skipped) EOL
        <> the same with empty lines

        do f1() <> a do statement, EOL skipped
        </
            here, do is not needed (println returns nothing), but in order to continue the body of the function, so that it does not end here,
            we will use do (a do statement can never be the last expression of a function)
        />
        do println("Hello!")

        def a: Int <> a declaration (or definition, in this case), EOL is skipped again
        a = readln().get_length() <> again, another declaration
        a + <> the + awaits to be complete, EOL is skipped
        b
        + x <> this + can only be complete if it follows the previous expression, so, for unary - use (-x) when written at the beginning of a line

    ::: f4 could also be written like this...
    f5(x) = use std.io.stdOut .readln, .println;;do f1();do println("Hello!")
    def a: Int; a = readln().get_length();   a + ; b +x

    <> ...but do not do it please! If the body of the function gets long, it is better to wrap it in parentheses, like with this main function

    ::: the formatted function without comments would look like this:
    f6(x)=
        use std.io.stdOut .readln, .println

        do f1()
        do println("Hello!")

        def a: Int
        a = readln().get_length()
        a + b + c <> last expression returned


    ::: for more than one argument, functions can take tuples
    f6(x, y) = x + y

    ::: a lambda function
    lam1 = fun(x) = x + 1

    ::: formatted
    lam2 =
        fun(x) = x + 1

    ::: another one
    def lam3: Int -> Int
    lam3 = a => a * 5 <> called soft lambdas (the reason will be understood later), they take just one arg, as everything

    ::: another soft lambda
    lam4 =
        (x, y) => -21 * (x + y) <> still one argument, a tuple

    ::: a real/hard lambda with auto arguments in a tuple ($1 is the first, $2 the second...)
    lam5 =
        fun = <> auto arguments of a tuple, if you want a 0 arguments lambda, use "fun() ="
            $0 + $1 <> using the pipe, which has low precedence so that you can connect code with operators, like here (passing the result of +)
            |> fun = $$0 + 234 <> every nested auto-args lambda gains one $ in its auto arguments

    ::: Multiline can be done like this too, with single line doc after other.
    ::: Hard lambdas can call themselves lambda, but only the inner one (soft ones can not be recursive at all, that is why they can be called soft).
    ::: Here we can see how auto expression completion is helpful to write minimalist functions (remember indentation does not matter!)
    factorial(n) =
        (1, n) |> <> again, this expects an expression, so it skips the EOL, like above |> at the beginning linked to the previous expression
        fun(acc, num) =
            match num with
            | 1 => acc <> ends with EOL
            | else =>
                lambda(acc * num, num - 1) <> ends with EOL, after skipping a line to complete the arrow

    use std.io.stdOut.println

    ::: nested match: how to differentiate whether a pattern expression belongs to one or another? by default, to the deepest one
    nested_match(x: Int) =
        match x with
        | Int<12 => "less than 12"
        | if x >= 12 && <= 66 => <> * see the comment after this function
            match x { <> if curlies are found (no matter the space or EOLs between), the parentheses delimit the body of the match expression
            | in 12, 13, 14, 15, ...[6, 7, 8, 9].map(n => n + 10) => "number between 12 and 19, '| in' calls the .has method of the object received,"
                ++ " otherwise we would have matched against a tuple or an array"
            | if [20..:(10)101].map(d => ...([0..==9].map(n => d + n))).has(x) => "20-100"
            }
        | if > 66 => "greater than 66" <> again, a branch in the outer match (and see **)

    </
        *:
        && followed by >, >=, <, <=, ==, !=, ~== or ¬ takes the first element of the previous expression (with the priority level of &&) to the middle,
        so x < y && > z is understood as (x < y) && (x > z), it is just syntactic sugar
        **:
        The use of >, >=, <, <=, ==, !=, or ~== as prefix compares with the object of the match, so that you do not need to write a lot, so
        match x with | if > k => (whatever) is like match x with | if x > k => (whatever)
    />

    ::: equivalent to the previous nested match
    nested_match2(x: Int) =
        match x with
        | Int<12 => "less than 12"
        | if x >= 12 && <= 66 => (
            match x with
            | in 12, 13, 14, 15, ...[6, 7, 8, 9].map(n => n + 10) => "number between 12 and 19"
            | if [20..:(10)101].map(d => ...([0..==9].map(n => d + n))).has(x) => "20-100"
        )
        | if > 66 => "greater than 66"

    :/
        an example of lambda in which we want to call the outer lambda (stored in a value so that we can still refer to it in the nested lambda)
        notice how we get rid of unnecessary parentheses: the body seems delimited by space like in Python, but it is not (safer against copy-paste errors)
    /:
    collatz(number): Int -> Nat64 =
        is_even(n) = n % 2 == 0
        is_odd(n) =
            n % 2 == 1

        ::: a bridge value
        num =
            if([1, 2, 4, -1, -2, -4].has(number)) return 0 <> early return from the function
            else
                number </
                        the result of the if expression as a whole is passed to the pipe,
                        although return is an early return to avoid the strictness of the last expression,
                        so, that branch, is known to scape the pipe (returns from the function, i.e. collatz)
                    />
            |> __.abs() <> we refer in a pipe to the value passed through as __, here we take the abs using a function/method from Int (Int64)

        even_func = fun(times: Int, n: Nat128): (Int, Nat128) -> Int = <> with the full type written
            even = lambda <> we store the current lambda
            n2 = n / 2
            match n2 with
            | in 1, 2, 4 => times
            | if is_even(n2) => lambda(times + 1, n2)
            | if is_odd(n2) => (
                fun(times2, nn) :-> Int =
                    n3 = 3 * nn + 1
                    match n3 with
                    | in 1, 2, 4 => times2
                    | if is_even(n3) => even(times2 + 1, n3)
                    | if is_odd(n3) => lambda(times2 + 1, n3)
            )(times + 1, n2) <> we need to use parentheses to wrap the lambda and call it right after its definition
        if(is_even num) even_func(0, num) else even_func(-1, 2 * num) <> last expression returned, the if expression in this case

    <> Now, we will see how to write types in functions (it is the same for hard lambdas, just consider fun the name of the function)

    ::: you can do it like this, taking on account thin short arrows are associative by the right
    def g1: Int -> Int -> Int <> is the same as def g1: Int -> (Int -> Int)
    g1(x) = fun(y) = x + y
    println(g1 int1 int2)

    ::: this is the type of the function
    g2(x) : Int -> (Int, Int) = (x, x ** 2)

    ::: now, the type of the argument
    g3(x: Int) = (x, x ** 2)

    ::: everything written!
    g4(x: Int) : Int -> (Int, Int) = (x, x - 12234)

    ::: you can write the return type
    g5(x) :-> Int = 22 * x

    ::: another example
    g6(x) : Int -> Int = x * 5

    ::: type of an argument in a lambda
    g7 =
        fun(x: Int) = x + 123

    ::: ...while here we can see the type of the function too
    g8(x: Int) :-> Int -> Int =
        <> remember: in lambdas, it works the same way
        fun(y) : Int -> Int = x * y

    ::: for lambdas with auto arguments, the type is the one of the lambda as well
    g9 =
        fun : Int -> Int = $0 + 3


    <><><> arrays and tuples

    ::: an array
    a1: [Int, Int, Int] = [1, 2, 3]

    ::: with type
    a2: [Int, Int, Bool] = [1, 2, true]

    elem1 = a1[0] <> the first element is at index 0
    elem2 =
        elem = a1[1]
        match elem with
        | Int => elem
        | else =>
            do println("this case will never happen!")
            0 <> returns 0

    ::: for long sizes, the type is written as the lenght in square brackets with a colon at the beginning
    a3: [:10]Int = [0] ** 10

    ::: any size (has a little overhead since it retains its size in runtime sometimes)
    anySizedArray: []Int = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] <> this type is more useful in functions...

    ::: ...like this one
    double_elements_in_array_of_any_size(arr: []Int) =
        arr.map(n => 2 * n)

    do double_elements_in_array_of_any_size(a1)             <> [2, 4, 6]
    do double_elements_in_array_of_any_size(anySizedArray)  <> [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]


    ::: a tuple is an "open" array
    t1 = (1, 2, 3)

    ::: indeed, to spread/open an array, use the spread operator
    t2: (Int, Int, Int) = ...a1

    ::: in the type, the spread works the same
    t3: ...[:2]Int = ...[1, 2]

    ::: and access is just the same as for normal arrays, which can be called bricks (because [...] looks like a brick)
    println t3[1]

    ::: nested tuples get plain
    a7: ...[:5]Int = (1, 2, (3, 4), 5) <> evals to (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)

    ::: to turn a tuple into a brick, just wrap it in brackets
    a8: [:3]Int = [t2]

    ::: this will evaluate to true
    thisIsTrue: Bool =
        [1, 2, 3, 3 + 1] == [(1, 2, (3, 4))]

    ::: the type of the array can be left to inference, just specifying the length
    a9: [:2] = [1, 2]

    ::: or no type, of course (thanks to inference)
    a10 = [0, ...a9, 3] <> this will eval to [0, 1, 2, 3] : [:4]Int

    ::: by .[ ] we can return a new version of an array
    a11 = a9.[3, 4] <> a11 is [3, 4]

    a12 = a9.[9] <> a12 is [9, 2]

    ::: in the array (both for values and for the type), we can refer the index by .[:indx]
    a13 = a9.[.[:1] = 87] <> a13 is [1, 87]

    ::: as said, in types it works too
    a14: [.[:1] : Int] = [true, -4, false] <> the type is therefore [Bool, Int, Bool], and a14[1] is Int

    <> ERROR: a15 = a13.[1, 2, 3] Out of range!

    <> use ++ to extend arrays

    a15: [:4]Int = a11 ++ a12
    a16 = [3, 4, 9, 2]
    do a15 == a16 <> evals to true

    ::: use [:] to express an array with some size (unlike []), inferred
    a17: [:]Int = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] <> a17: [:5]Int


    <><><> array fields and structs

    ::: arrays can have named fields so that we do not need to write or remember the index of what we want to get
    s1 = [0, .field = 1] <> not all fields must have name, sometimes we just need an array with an special position named

    ::: the same in the type
    s2: [Int, .field: Int] = s1 <> the fields must be named the same

    <> and access works both with index and with name:
    do s1[0]
    do s1[1]
    do s1[-1] <> like in python, reverse indexing
    do s1.field
    do s2.field
    do s2[1]

    ::: names can be nested
    s3 = [
        .a: Int = 1, <> a name with type
        ::: documentation for the field
        .b = 2,
        3, <> no name
        .c = [1, 2, 3], <> can be heterogeneus, but access will be a bit slower due to boxing in some cases (or the array might be bigger because of padding)
        .d: [:2]Int = 4, <> the type is not neccesary
        .d = 5, <> .d has now two elements (if extended like this, a field can only be next to itself, so [.a, .b, .a] is not allowed)
        .e = 6 : Int, <> we can write the type of the expression (here, number 6), to refer to that one element of a field, here .e
        .e: [...[:2]Int, .a: Int] = 7, <> yes, the spread works in types too, and it is the type of `e`, not of e[1]
        .e.a = 8,
        9 <> a number
    ]

    <> it works this way:
    do s3.e.a <> 8

    ::: the syntax for new versions is the same
    s4: [:]Int = <> with [:] we let the length be inferred
        s3.[
        2, <> positional
        3, <> positional as well, does not matter it has a field name
        .e.a = -512, <> by field, positional redefinition stops
        .[:2] = 6, <> by index
        .c = 9, <> by field again
        .d.[:1] = 65 <> by the index in a field
    ]

    ::: in types, you can add a field to an index, here after .e.a, in index 9, we place the .f field
    s5: [.[:9]->.f: Int] = s3 <> gains the field name, since it is a cast to a type whose index 9 has field f

    ::: or...
    s5_2 = s3.[.[:9]->.f]

    ::: a type alias
    use CisAnArrayRestInts =
        [:10]Int.[ <> the same way we get a new version from an array with values, we can do it with types
            .[:s3.c.__offset__()] = [:3]Int <> we change the value of the element at s3.c.__offset__(), which here was of type Type
        ]

    ::: you can also erase the fields, here, coerced to an array without fields,
    ::: created in a fancy way to express "array of Ints except the element in the position of .c"
    s6: CFieldPositionisAnArrayRestInts = s3


    ::: if you want an array where every element has a field, use
    @[structArray]
    s7 = [.a = 1, .b = 2, .c = 3]

    ::: if you want the element to be just accessed by the name of the field, use
    @[struct]
    s8 = s7 <> the cast works, since every element in s7 has a name


    <><><> some control structures

    <><> if and its conditionals

    <> if-parentheses, useful for inline if expressions

    do if(true) 1 else 2

    do
        if(true) <> when the first token after `if` is a parentheses, it is an if-parenthesis
            x = 1
            y = if(true) 2 else 3
            x + y <> until a next clause, such as else, is a block, whose last expression is returned
        else
            89 - 102122 <> could be written as well like `else 89 - 102122`

    do if ( <> together is the correct format to differentiate this form from others, although if SPACE (...) is valid too
            aux(n: Int) = n.is_odd()
            aux([1, 2, 3].fold((acc, n) => acc + n))
        ) <> there can be a block with many expressions, although it is not recommended
        true else 4 <> the else body ends when its expression finishes...
        + 5 <> ...with EOL in this case, after completing the + operation

    <> the previous one would look better like this:
    do if(
        aux(n: Int) = n.is_odd()
        aux([1, 2, 3].fold((acc, n) => acc + n))
    ) true else 4 + 5

    <> if-curly: good for large, multiline bodies

    do if true { <> if the first token is NOT a parenthesis
        1
    } else ( <> parentheses are useful to extend the else to more than one line
        x = 2
        x * 54
    )

    do if true {
        1
    } else { <> YES, you can use curly braces after an else too (recommended for consistency)
        x = 2
        x * 54
    }

    <> the if-curly syntax takes as the condition whatever it is before the curly, like a block
    do if
        aux(n: Int) = n.is_odd()
        aux([1, 2, 3].fold((acc, n) => acc + n)) {
            true
        } else 4 + 5

    <> if-parentheses is better for one line, while if-curly for multiline or nested

    <> elif: works the same, accepting both syntactic forms

    do if(true) 1 elif(false) 2 else 3

    do if true {
        1
    } elif false {
        2
    } else {
        3
    }

    <> andif
    <> it is only evaluated if the previous condition was satisfied, avoiding an extra indent

    do if true {
        1
    } andif true {
        2
    } else 3

    <> without andif...
    do if true {
        if true {
            2
        } else {
            1 <> if false, the last value before andif is retuned
        }
    } else 3

    <> andif can be after an elif clause too, and have its andelifs and andelse
    do if true {
        1
    } elif true {
        2
    } andif true {
        3
    } elif true { <> if elif was not executed
        do 4
    } andif true {
        5
    } andelif true { <> if the previous condition have not found true, but still inside elif
        6
    } andelif true {
        7
    } andelse {
        8
    } else { <> we still need an else for the main if expression
        9
    }

    <> without andconditionals
    do if true {
        1
    } elif true {
        if true {
            3
        } else {
            2
        }
    } elif true { <> if elif was not executed
        do 4
        if true {
            5
        } elif true {
            6
        } elif true {
            7
        } else {
            8
        }
    } else { <> we still need an else for the main if expression
        9
    }

    </
        IMPORTANT: and-contitionals do NOT accept if-parenthesis, since if there are many conditions,
        it is likely that they are not in a single line, complying with the if-curly format therefore.
    />

    <><> doif: when an if has not an else (works as a statement from Java or C)

    doif(true) println("hey") <> and there is no else!

    doif true { println("hey") } <> works

    doif true {
        println("hey")
    } doelif true {
        println("works as you expect")
    } anddoelif true {
        println("yes, anddoelif exists")
    } anddoelse {
        println("the else for the doelif")
    } doelif true { <> no and, so it is the elif for the main doif
        println("and no else! ends at EOL")
    }

    doif(true) println("hey") doelse println("doelse exists as well, although it makes no sense,"
        ++ " since with a single do and then a standard if-conditional, it is the same as a doif with an else")

    <><> value-ret conditionals

    do 5??{false} <> if the value is none, returns false

    <> multiconditional...
    do 5??{
        false <> the first case is triggered if the value is none
        orif 4 => false
        orif 6 => false <> if there is no else, and nothing matches, returns the original value (i.e. 5 in this case)
    }

    do 5??{
        false
        orif 4 => false
        orif 5 => true
        orif 6 => false
        else => "else case" <> with an else, everything will match (or the else case), working as some sort of switch
    }

    <> we can capture the value, useful if it is the result of a complex expression we do not want to repeat
    complex_operation(n) = n + 21

    do (3 * complex_operation(-66))??::nameOfTheValue{
        false
        orif 45 => false
        orif 5 | -155 => true
        orif -45 => false
        else => nameOfTheValue + 1
    }

    <><> when conditional: like if, but in a different order

    do true when 2 == 3 - 1 else false <> until EOL

    do true when <> until else, it is the body
        is_even(n) = n % 2 == 0
        is_even(4)
    else false

    <> another option
    do true when(2 == 3 - 1) false <> like having an else right after the last parenthesis

    </
        In maths, sometimes we write functions like this:
        abs_div(x) =
            -1 / x  when x < 0
            0       when x == 0
            1 / x   when x > 0

        In Monom, it is similar:
    />

    <> (else() is the same as else none)
    abs_div1(x)=
        -1 / x  when x < 0  else
        0       when x == 0 else
        1 / x   when x > 0  else()

    ::: even better
    abs_div2(x) =
        -1 / x  when(x < 0)
        0       when(x == 0)
        1 / x   when(x > 0) none

    <> remember, else allows curly braces after it

    <><> for loop

    ls = [1, 2, 3, 4]

    for i in ls do { println(i) }

    for elem in ls.val_index_pairs() do {
        (val, index) = elem
        println(i"\(index) = \(val)")
    }

    for i in ls while i < 3 do {
        println(i"\(i) less than 3")
    }

    for i in ls until i >= 3 do {
        println(i"\(i) less than 3")
    }

    total = for(acc: Int = 0) i in ls return acc do {
        println("sum")
        for.call(acc + i)
    }

    forever do {
        println("forever!!!")
    }

    for(i = 0) while i < 10 do {
        println(i)
        for.call(i + 1)
    }

    for i in ls do {
        doif(i == 2) for.break()
        println(i)
    }

    for::my_loop i in ls do {
        doif(i == 2) my_loop.break()
        println(i)
    }

    for::outer i in ls do {
        println(i)
        for j in ls do {
            println(": " ++ i)
            domatch j with <> domatch, a not exhaustive match
            | 2 => for.break()
            | 3 => outer.break()
        }
    }

    ::: some things together...
    fib_sum(number) =
        for(a: Int = 0, b: Int = 1, times = 0, total = 0)
        until times >= number <> unlike with while, the condition of until is not evaluated before starting the loop
        return total do {
            println("for arguments must be within parentheses, it is how the parser know you will declare arguments")
            println("(which must have a default value, as well as the return value)")
            n = a + b
            for.call(b, n, times + 1, total + n)
        }

    ::: for loops are understood as a function, so they can be called recursively, and the arguments are passed to the next iteration
    ::: the same with lambdas:
    fib_sum_with_lambdas(number) =
        fib(a: Int, b: Int, acc: Int = 0, times: Int) =
            if(times >= number) acc else (
                n = a + b
                fib(b, n, acc + n, times + 1)
            )
        fib(0, 1, 0, 0)

    <> with arguments and iterator
    for(previous: Int = 0, preprevious: Int = 0) i in ls do {
        println(i"\(i) -> pre: \(previous), prepre: \(preprevious), total: \(previous + preprevious)")
        <> ERROR: previous = preprevious <> reasignation of a constant, NOT valid
        for.call(preprevious, i)
    }

    <> if there are arguments, each iteration is called with the same as before, unless you pass the new one
    for(i = 1, c: Bool = false) while i < 20 do {
        println(i)
        doif(i == 4 && not c) for.call(c = true) <> i remains the same
    }

    <><><> more on functions

    <> functions can be overloaded, and be misfix (by parts)

    <><> overloading (depending on types and number of arguments, like in Java)

    <> syntactic sugar: if you write `:,` it means your type is one one of the next identifier, helpful in functions

    ::: when overloading, a tag must be written, to differentiate the versions (if no tag is written, the function can NOT be overloaded)
    add(x:, y: Int) 'i_i' : (Int, Int) -> Int = x + y <> (both x and y are Int)

    ::: another with different type
    add(a: Int, y: Float64) 'i_f' : (Int, Float64) -> Float64 = x + y

    ::: different number of arguments
    add(x:, y:, z: Int) 'i_i_i' : (Int, Int, Int) -> Int = x + y + z

    do add(1, 2)
    do add(1.0, 2.0)
    do (1, 2, 3)

    ::: an empty tag means the function can be overloaded, and that refering the function means by default this one
    func_() '' = println("I can have more versions")

    ::: the rest must have a tag (usually refering the type or the function)
    func_(x: Int) 'prints_x' = println(i"\(x)")

    testing_function(f: Thing -> Thing, arg: Thing) =
        if(args.Type is_from f.Arg) f(...arg) <> ...Int is Int, we use it to comply with tuples and with single calls
        else
            do println("ERROR")
            none

    do testing_function(add.'i_i', [1, 2]) <> we can NOT use just add, since there are many
    do testing_function(func_, none) <> we CAN do this because its tag is empty (useful for letting others overload your function protecting it)
    do testing_function(func_.'prints_x', -756) <> now needs the tag

    <><> mixfix functions

    ::: a mixfix function has its identifier by parts, which can be called connectors
    ::: a tag should be written, it is how you can refer the function
    ::: (if no tag is given, the automatic one would be 'mult__by__', but if it already exists,
    ::: YOU must write the tag, since the automatic one already exists)

    mult(x: Int)by(y: Int) 'mult_by' = x * y

    do [[1, 2], [3, 4]].map(elem => mult.'mult_by'(...elem)) <> the tag belongs to the first identifier

    </
        The beginning must be an identifier and must end with arguments. These are NOT valid:
        (x)and_sum(y) = x + y <> ERROR: function names must start with an identifier

        execute(f: f32, value: f32)three_times = f(f(f(value))) <> ERROR: functions must end with a call
    />

    ::: mixfix functions can be overloaded too
    ::: the empty tag is like the automatic one
    div(x: Int)plus(y: Int)by(z: Int) '' = (x + y) / if(z == 0) 1 else z

    ::: each combination can NOT have the same type as other (div(Int) is not valid again)
    div(x: Float32)by(y: Int) 'f_i' = x / if(y == 0) 1 else y

    ::: however, if a type changes, the rest is new too
    div(x: Float32)plus(y: Int)by(z: Int) 'f_i_i' = (x + y) / if(z == 0) 1 else z

    ::: the not-mixfix one can exist too
    div(x:, y: Int) 'simple' : (Int, Int) -> Float = x.div_by(y) <> a safe method, returns NaN if there is a problem

    do (
        testing_function(div, [1, 2, 3]), <> returns 1.0, div is div.'' if there is a version with empty tag
        testing_function(div.'simple', [1, 2]) <> returns 0.5
    )

    do (
        div(1)plus(2)by(3),
        div(1.0)by(2),
        div(1.0)plus(2)by(3),
        div(1, 2)
        div.'simple'(1, 2)
    )


    <> In any case, both overloading and mixfix, the mangled name is the first identifier plus __ and the tag (nothing if empty)
    <> add__i_i, add__i_f, add__i_i_i, func___, func___prints_x, mult__mult_by, div__, div__f_i, div__f_i_i, div__simple
    <> you can use @[mangle_as] to create your mangled name

    @[mangle_as("my_mult")]
    mult(x:, y: Int) 'mult2' = x * y

    do testing_function(mult.'mult2', [1, 2]) <> you still use the tag, the mangling is in the binary file, the compilation symbol
)